1
00:00:00,800 --> 00:00:07,040
Polars 2.0 introduces a Map data type, for objects whose keys are data rather than part of the type.

2
00:00:08,660 --> 00:00:12,840
Polars-genson infers the schema of JSON data, including Map types.

3
00:00:14,880 --> 00:00:17,700
In some objects, the keys are part of the type.

4
00:00:18,760 --> 00:00:21,420
A person's name and date of birth are fields:

5
00:00:21,940 --> 00:00:25,080
every person has them, and they hold different types of value:

6
00:00:26,100 --> 00:00:28,180
one's a string and one's a date.

7
00:00:28,760 --> 00:00:32,000
They can't share one value type, so you'd store them in a struct.

8
00:00:35,460 --> 00:00:41,520
In other objects, the keys are data, like these labels from Wikidata, with one label for each language.

9
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:50,920
Wikidata has labels in hundreds of languages, and each city has labels in only some of them, and every label is a string.

10
00:00:50,920 --> 00:00:52,940
So that's a map.

11
00:00:57,040 --> 00:01:08,120
If you stored these as a struct, with a field for every language, most of the values would be null, and every new language would add another field to the type.

12
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:21,660
If you store them as a map instead, Paris has five labels and Rome has just two, and there are no nulls anywhere.

13
00:01:22,400 --> 00:01:27,640
The column is a map with string keys and string values, however many languages there are.

14
00:01:31,620 --> 00:01:38,240
In Polars, a map's keys can be of any type that's row-encodable, which means anything except Object.

15
00:01:39,360 --> 00:01:43,920
JSON object keys are always strings, so polars-genson's maps have string keys.

16
00:01:48,040 --> 00:01:50,980
polars-genson infers a schema from your JSON.

17
00:01:51,520 --> 00:01:54,680
You can choose which fields become maps with force_field_types.

18
00:01:59,740 --> 00:02:03,460
Or you can let it decide from the data.

19
00:02:03,940 --> 00:02:10,260
An object whose values all share one type and that has more distinct keys than map_threshold becomes a map.

20
00:02:12,200 --> 00:02:17,120
The default threshold is 20, so these labels, with 8 keys, stay a struct.

21
00:02:18,340 --> 00:02:20,420
Lower it to 5, and they become a map.

22
00:02:25,860 --> 00:02:28,120
Polars has functions for working with Map columns.

23
00:02:28,720 --> 00:02:30,625
map.get looks up a key:

24
00:02:30,625 --> 00:02:34,820
here, English, and here, Spanish, which two of the cities don't have.

25
00:02:35,820 --> 00:02:37,780
And map.len counts each city's labels.

26
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:45,560
polars-genson 1.0 supports Polars 2.0, and you can install it from PyPI.

27
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:47,300
See the docs to get started.
